  "teacher10_actual_before_IL": {
    "when": "When ILLINOIS is allowable and the debtor owns personal-use personal property.",
    "procedure": "Use two independent passes. First add 735 Ill. Comp. Stat. 5/12-1001(b) for every personal-use personal-property asset, including apparel, a vehicle, or a prescribed health aid; the words “any other property” do not make (b) mutually exclusive with a specific alternative. Its whole-asset amount is the smaller of the asset value and $4,000 for one debtor or $8,000 for two equal joint debtors. Second add each factually matching specific provision: (a) for necessary wearing apparel, (c) for a motor vehicle with a $2,400 asset-level ceiling, (d) only for facts stating trade use with a $1,500 ceiling, and (e) for a professionally prescribed health aid. Provisions (a) and (e) retain the asset value because they state no dollar ceiling.",
    "avoid_when": "Do not apply these alternatives to property stated to be business-use. Do not omit (b) when a specific provision applies. Do not infer (d) from the object's form, name, tool loops, or possible usefulness; the facts must establish use in the debtor's trade. Do not double the (c) ceiling or classify jewelry as necessary wearing apparel.",
    "operators": [],
    "jurisdiction": "ILLINOIS"
  },
  "teacher10_actual_proposed_IL": {
    "when": "When ILLINOIS is allowable and the debtor owns personal-use personal property.",
    "procedure": "Complete two recorded passes for each asset. Pass one: add 735 Ill. Comp. Stat. 5/12-1001(b) to every personal-use personal-property asset, including every motor vehicle, apparel item, and prescribed health aid; use the smaller of the whole asset value and $4,000 for one debtor or $8,000 for two equal joint debtors. Pass two: independently add each matching specific provision—(a) for necessary wearing apparel, (c) for a motor vehicle capped at $2,400 for the whole asset, (d) only when the facts state trade use capped at $1,500, and (e) for a professionally prescribed health aid. Provisions (a) and (e) retain the whole asset value because they state no dollar ceiling. Before finalizing, verify that every qualifying vehicle contains both (b) and (c).",
    "avoid_when": "Do not apply these alternatives to property stated to be business-use. Never treat (b) and a specific provision as mutually exclusive or omit (b) from a vehicle. Do not infer (d) from an object's form, name, tool loops, or possible usefulness; the facts must establish trade use. Do not double the (c) ceiling or classify jewelry as necessary wearing apparel.",
    "operators": [],
    "jurisdiction": "ILLINOIS"
  },
